            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Opposition to Julius Caesar</strong><br>\nCato became one of Caesar's staunchest opponents, objecting to his unconventional tactics and concentration of power. He often spoke against Caesar in the Senate and sought to rally the senatorial class to defend the Republic's traditional institutions. Cato's fear was that Caesar's reforms would lead to tyranny and the end of the Roman Republic.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Role in the Civil War</strong><br>\nDuring the Roman Civil War, Cato supported Pompey against Caesar. He took an active role in the conflict, serving as a commander in several campaigns. Cato's military leadership was characterized by the same discipline and austerity that he displayed in his political life. His dedication to the Republic's cause was unwavering, even in the face of impending defeat.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Financial Reforms</strong><br>\nCato used his position to advocate for financial reforms that would benefit the state and reduce corruption. He pushed for more transparent governance and for the Senate to have greater control over financial matters. He believed that Rome's financial stability was key to maintaining its republican structure and preventing the concentration of power in the hands of a few individuals.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
