          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Virtue and Public Service</strong><br>\nCato's life was a testament to the idea that personal virtue was essential for public service. He avoided excess in his personal life and denounced the luxurious lifestyle of the elite as corrupting and against the interests of the public. Cato believed that only by maintaining a simple and virtuous life could a politician honestly serve the people and the Republic.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Roman Politics</strong><br>\nCato's political career had a lasting impact on Roman politics. His unwavering commitment to the Republic's principles influenced many of his contemporaries and those who came after him. His actions and policies were driven by his desire to see the Republic survive and prosper, and he became a symbol of republican values.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nCato's legacy was solidified by his dramatic end; he chose suicide over living under Caesar's rule, which he believed would be the death of the Roman Republic. His death was seen by many as the ultimate act of defiance against tyranny and has been remembered as a powerful statement of republican virtue and autonomy. Cato's staunch morality and political ideals continued to influence Roman and Western political thought long after his death.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
